{"type":"assistant","uuid":"20af6f1e-b726-4344-bf22-be3b219c688c","session_id":"b2f6e5c0-de77-46a8-a4c0-eed00bd27758","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"20af6f1e-b726-4344-bf22-be3b219c688c","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The fix is in place. Let me provide a summary of the bug fix.## Summary\n\n**Bug:** The `NumberFormatException` crash when trying to open the add/remove medicine stock dialog. The actual crash was:```java\njava.lang.IllegalStateException: Fragment b{f3e5022 #0 number_dialog} declared target fragment MedicineCreateOrEditFragment{...} that does not belong to this FragmentManager!```\n\n**Root Cause:** In `MedicineCreateOrEditFragment.showStockDialog()`, the code used `setTargetFragment(this)` on the `NumberPickerBuilder` to set up the callback. However, when `NumberPickerDialogFragment` (a child fragment shown via `getChildFragmentManager()`) tried to set itself as having a target fragment that points to `MedicineCreateOrEditFragment`, Android's FragmentManager validation rejected it because the target fragment relationship was causing a cross-FragmentManager dependency issue. Specifically, when the dialog fragment is shown as a child fragment of `MedicineCreateOrEditFragment` (which itself is inside a ViewPager in the Activity), the `setTargetFragment()` call creates a validation conflict.\n \n**Solution:** I switched to using `addNumberPickerDialogHandler(this)` instead, which registers the callback handler through a direct listener mechanism rather than relying on fragment target relationships. The `NumberPickerDialogFragment.NumberPickerDialogHandlerV2` callback was already implemented in the fragment, so this approach avoids the cross-FragmentManager validation issue entirely. The project builds successfully with this change.\n","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0}}}
{"type":"assistant","uuid":"176f1b3f-9f71-42be-82e8-818be4d2b33d","session_id":"b2f6e5c0-de77-46a8-a4c0-eed00bd27758","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"176f1b3f-9f71-42be-82e8-818be4d2b33d","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Summary\n\n**Bug:** `IllegalStateException: Fragment ... declared target fragment MedicineCreateOrEditFragment ... that does not belong to this FragmentManager!` when tapping the '+' or '-' stock buttons.\n\n**Root Cause:** In `MedicineCreateOrEditFragment.showStockDialog()`, the code used `.setTargetFragment(this)` on the `NumberPickerBuilder`. This creates a target-fragment relationship between the `NumberPickerDialogFragment` (shown via `getChildFragmentManager()`) and `MedicineCreateOrEditFragment`. Android's FragmentManager rejects this relationship when the fragment is hosted in a ViewPager because the cross-fragment-manager validation fails.\n\n**Fix:** Replaced `.setTargetFragment(this)` with `.addNumberPickerDialogHandler(this)`. This uses the library's callback registration API instead of the fragment-target API, which avoids the FragmentManager validation issue entirely.\n\n**The change:**\n```diff\n-                        .setTargetFragment(this).setReference(ref)\n+                        .addNumberPickerDialogHandler(this).setReference(ref)\n```\n\nThe class already implements `NumberPickerDialogFragment.NumberPickerDialogHandlerV2` and has the required `onDialogNumberSet()` callback method, so no other changes are needed.\n\nThe project builds successfully with this fix."}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":85246,"output_tokens":575,"cache_read_input_tokens":84480,"total_tokens":85821}}}
